Looking at the graph, the curve crosses the x-axis at four points: x = -3, x = -1, x = 1, and x = 3. Between these crossings the curve dips below the axis (around x = ±2) and rises to a local maximum at (0, 1), but it only touches zero at those four x-values. All four lie within [-6, 6], and there are no other intersections with the x-axis in that interval (the curve stays above the axis beyond |x| > 3).

Therefore, the number of distinct real zeros is:

\boxed{4}
